    "body": "## Problem\n\nWith `jumpoptions+=view`, jump-list entries save the relative topline and\nhorizontal skip, but not filler lines above the topline. If virtual lines are\ndisplayed above the first buffer line, jumping away and back restores the\ncursor and topline while dropping those virtual lines from the saved view.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThe behavior was reproduced on Neovim `master` at\n[`ca992e82d4`](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/commit/ca992e82d40cb8ed31331ba75cf0a58c6d693ac7).\n\nStart Nvim with `nvim --clean`, then run:\n\n```vim\n:set jumpoptions=view\n:call setline(1, range(1, 30))\n:lua ns = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('jump-view-topfill')\n:lua vim.api.nvim_buf_set_extmark(0, ns, 0, 0, { virt_lines = { { { 'virtual line' } } }, virt_lines_above = true })\n:lua vim.fn.winrestview({ topline = 1, topfill = 1 })\n```\n\nAt this point, `:lua print(vim.fn.winsaveview().topfill)` prints `1`. Press\n`G`, then `<C-o>`, and inspect it again.\n\nBefore this patch:\n\n```text\n0\n```\n\nAfter this patch:\n\n```text\n1\n```\n\nForward and backward jump-list navigation now round-trips the complete view.\n\n## Solution\n\nStore `w_topfill` in `fmarkv_T` together with the topline offset and skip\ncolumn. Restore it after setting the topline, then call `check_topfill()` to\nvalidate it against the current window state.\n\nAdd a functional test covering virtual lines above the first buffer line and\nboth `<C-o>` and `<C-i>` navigation.\n\nThe functional test can be run with:\n\n```sh\nmake functionaltest TEST_FILE=test/functional/editor/jump_spec.lua\n```\n\n## AI disclosure\n\nAI tools assisted with analyzing the behavior and preparing the implementation\nand test. The commit includes the generic `AI-assisted` token.\n",
